{
	title: 'Sessions in one directory hear who edits what',
	spec: 'Sessions in one cwd hear which project files other open sessions there declared, so agents can share a checkout. A bash call reports its declared modifies paths when it starts (fileChanges.begin, task 8w). Before each model request (history.messages), a session gets one notice line per other session with paths it has not been told: "[158-abc (Fix render) declared edits to src/a.ts, src/b.ts]" (at most 5 paths, then "N more"). Only relative project paths count: absolute paths, /tmp, ~ and Git internals (.git/...) are never sent. A path is told to each reader once; a path not declared again for 5 minutes is free and forgotten, so a later declaration is told again. When a session ends a turn with a final answer (not a <question>, a pause or an error), each reader told some of its paths gets "[158-abc (Fix render) finished its turn: src/a.ts]" for those paths only. Notes report declared activity; files have no owners. Closed sessions are forgotten. State is in memory: after a host restart first notes repeat. Module: src/host/neighbors.ts, fed by file-changes.ts and turns.ts.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'src/host/neighbors.ts: state.seen (session -> cwd + path -> last time) and state.sent (viewer|neighbor -> last note text without the age) are host-wide; record() is called from file-changes.ts begin and finish, append() from bash.ts. Neighbors must be in sessions.state.open. Declared globs appear as written. The note sits inside the tool output, so tools.cap can cut it with a very long output, but the tail is kept. Test uses the injected clock.now.',
